module extdeps.llm.cursor_cli

import std.types { String, NonEmptyStr, Bool, Int, List, FilePath, brand }
import extdeps.external_authority { ExternalAuthority }
import extdeps.uri { Uri, Https }
import extdeps.exec.program { ProgramIdentity }
import extdeps.exec.command { ArgvCommand, argv_command, argv_words }

data extdeps_external_authority_anchor: ExternalAuthority = ExternalAuthority {
  uri: Uri {
    scheme: Https
    locator: "docs.cursor.com/en/cli/overview"
  }
}

// The one program-token authority for the Cursor agent CLI, the sibling of codex_cli_program and
// claude_cli_program. Observed installed on srv2 at ~/.local/bin/cursor-agent; every surface below
// was read from that binary's own --help and --list-models output on 2026-07-30 rather than from
// documentation, so the rows describe the interface actually present on the host.

data cursor_cli_program: String = "cursor-agent"

// CURSOR AUTHENTICATES WITH A VALUE, WHICH MAKES IT THE EASY ONE. --api-key takes the key directly and CURSOR_API_KEY is the environment equivalent, so there is no interactive flow and no device code, and a key placed in a secret store can be materialized at the point of invocation. That does NOT make the invocation stateless: a keyed invocation persists the value and derived tokens under the standard HOME/XDG config root (cursor/auth.json, observed 2026-10-17, see below), so the caller must supply and own an isolated runtime root.\n\nThe structural difference from codex is narrower than it first looked. codex authenticates into a DIRECTORY — CODEX_HOME selects an isolated store containing tokens with an hour-long access lifetime and an opaque durable root. Cursor accepts a value per invocation and has no provider-specific account-root selector comparable to CODEX_HOME, so the account axis is the key; the isolation mechanism is the generic HOME/XDG config root the caller binds. A model that assumed one isolation mechanism across providers would have to force one of them into the other's shape.\n\nA login subcommand also exists and writes into ~/.cursor, which is the interactive alternative. It is deliberately NOT modeled as the automation path: it produces host state that cannot be replicated from a secret store, which is exactly the property that makes codex's credential awkward to fleet-provision.
type CursorAuth
  = CursorApiKeyArgument { key: NonEmptyStr }
  | CursorApiKeyEnvironment { key: NonEmptyStr }

data cursor_api_key_env_var: NonEmptyStr = "CURSOR_API_KEY" as NonEmptyStr

// THERE IS NO CURSOR_CONFIG_DIR, and the absence is the fact worth recording. codex isolates
// accounts by state root and this CLI has no provider-specific environment variable for it — ~/.cursor
// holds agent-cli-state.json, cli-config.json and a chats directory for whichever account last logged in.
// The standard HOME/XDG_CONFIG_HOME roots do move the credential store (auth.json, below), which is why a
// keyed invocation must run under a root its caller owns.\n\nSo cursor's account axis is the API KEY,
// not a directory. Two accounts are two keys passed to two invocations; the credential each persists
// lands under the config root that invocation ran with, so a shared host root would hold both. That means concurrent sessions under different accounts are possible in a
// way codex's directory model does not require any care about, while concurrent sessions writing
// chat history to one shared directory is a contention question this module cannot answer and has
// not observed.\n\nRecorded rather than papered over: gunbc.provider_account's @label suffix scheme
// is a CODEX convention and does not transfer here. A cursor account selection is a secret
// reference, and forcing it into a state-root shape would invent a directory the tool does not
// read.

// --print is the non-interactive surface and it is the only mode that reaches the automation flags. Its own help states that print mode has access to all tools including write and shell, so it is the analogue of codex exec rather than a read-only dump. --output-format admits text, json or stream-json, and stream-json is the JSONL progress stream this repository already consumes from codex — so provider events are available on the same shape, from a different vendor.\n\n--trust is bound to the same mode: 'Trust the current workspace without prompting (only works with --print/headless mode)'. That is the same class of first-launch obstacle claude_workspace_trust_seeded already models for claude code, where an unseeded workspace blocks interactively and no permission flag covers it. Cursor exposes it as a flag rather than as config state, which is strictly easier — but it must be passed, and omitting it is how an unattended run hangs waiting for a human.
type CursorOutputFormat
  = CursorText
  | CursorJson
  | CursorStreamJson

fn cursor_output_format_wire(f: CursorOutputFormat) -> String {
  match f {
    CursorText => "text"
    CursorJson => "json"
    CursorStreamJson => "stream-json"
  }
}

// The mode axis is a CLOSED choice on the CLI's own surface: --mode admits plan (read-only,
// proposes without editing) and ask (read-only Q&A), while omitting it leaves the default editing
// behaviour. Modeled with an explicit default arm rather than an Optional, because 'no mode flag'
// is a real third behaviour and not the absence of information.\n\nSeparately, --force (aliased
// --yolo) allows commands unless explicitly denied, and --sandbox admits enabled or disabled while
// overriding config. Force and sandbox are ORTHOGONAL to mode and to each other, so they are
// separate fields: a read-only plan run needs neither, and an unattended editing run needs a
// deliberate decision about both. Fusing them into one permission enum would make the sensible
// combinations unrepresentable.

type CursorExecutionMode
  = CursorDefaultEditMode
  | CursorPlanMode
  | CursorAskMode

type CursorSandboxChoice
  = CursorSandboxFromConfig
  | CursorSandboxEnabled
  | CursorSandboxDisabled

fn cursor_mode_args(mode: CursorExecutionMode) -> List<String> {
  match mode {
    CursorDefaultEditMode => []
    CursorPlanMode => ["--mode", "plan"]
    CursorAskMode => ["--mode", "ask"]
  }
}

fn cursor_sandbox_args(choice: CursorSandboxChoice) -> List<String> {
  match choice {
    CursorSandboxFromConfig => []
    CursorSandboxEnabled => ["--sandbox", "enabled"]
    CursorSandboxDisabled => ["--sandbox", "disabled"]
  }
}

// THE SANDBOX HAS A FREE PREFLIGHT. `cursor-agent sandbox run <cmd> [args...]` runs one command in
// the sandbox a sandbox-enabled turn uses, and calls no provider and reads no key: the CLI's own
// `sandbox --help` (2026.09.15-d2fe57e, read on srv2 2026-10-16) gives it as "Run a command in a
// sandbox (defaults to workspace read/write)". Cursor documents the Linux sandbox as needing kernel
// 6.2 or later with Landlock v3 and unprivileged user namespaces, and documents falling back to
// approval-before-run when they are missing (cursor.com/docs/agent/run-modes, Sandboxing). Observed
// on srv2: a host that cannot build the sandbox prints the error class below on STDOUT and exits 1,
// and a sandbox-enabled --print turn on the same host fails the same way before any provider request.
//
// WHAT RUNS INSIDE IS A READ OF THE REALIZED ENVIRONMENT, NOT `true`. The same table documents
// CURSOR_SANDBOX as set to "native" on Linux when the process is running inside the sandbox. So the
// preflight's command prints that variable from inside: exit 0 alone could be a run the CLI let
// through unsandboxed, and only the subject's own read of the variable separates the two
// (gunbc.recurring_failure_mode suppressed_precondition_failure_runs_the_workload_unconstrained).
// What a host must provide to pass is the host's fact, not this module's.
data cursor_sandbox_unsupported_error_class: NonEmptyStr = "SandboxUnsupportedError" as NonEmptyStr
data cursor_sandbox_env_var: NonEmptyStr = "CURSOR_SANDBOX" as NonEmptyStr
data cursor_sandbox_linux_native_value: NonEmptyStr = "native" as NonEmptyStr

// THIS MODULE OWNS THE COMMAND SURFACE AND NO PLACEMENT. Every builder below takes the program from its
// caller, so which executable runs (an exact root-owned path, gunbc.host.cursor_agent_realization) is the
// caller's fact and there is no second, PATH-named route here. cursor_cli_program stays only as the
// CLI's own name, for a reader that matches a process by name rather than launching one.
//
// --disable-auto-update IS A HIDDEN GLOBAL OPTION ("Disable auto-updates"): absent from `--help`, accepted
// by the pinned 2026.09.15-d2fe57e build (exit 0 with --version and with status, srv2 2026-10-17). A
// root-owned pinned tree cannot be written by the invoking user, so every pinned invocation (preflight, key
// probe and turn) passes it and never attempts a self-update.
data cursor_disable_auto_update_flag: String = "--disable-auto-update"

// STATUS OBSERVES STORED AUTH, NOT THE KEY IN THE ENVIRONMENT. Observed on cursor-agent 2026.09.15-d2fe57e
// on srv2, 2026-10-17: `status` reports only the auth stored under the Cursor config root
// ($XDG_CONFIG_HOME/cursor/auth.json); under an empty config root an invalid CURSOR_API_KEY and the
// valid custody key both read unauthenticated. So it answers "does this config root hold stored auth",
// and no builder here claims it judges a supplied key.
//
// --list-models VALIDATES THE INVOCATION'S ENVIRONMENT KEY (same build, same day). An invalid
// CURSOR_API_KEY exits 1 with stderr "The provided API key is invalid. The API key was loaded from the
// CURSOR_API_KEY environment variable"; the valid custody key exits 0 and prints "Available models"
// followed by model rows. It establishes that the CLI accepts the credential and nothing about quota,
// entitlement or a paid turn's success.
//
// A KEYED INVOCATION PERSISTS THE KEY. After one --list-models under an empty XDG_CONFIG_HOME,
// cursor/auth.json appeared there with the shape {accessToken, refreshToken, apiKey}. Whatever config
// root an invocation sees therefore receives the credential, so a caller that runs a keyed invocation
// owns that root's lifetime (gunbc.cursor_harness_credential); this module owns only the fact.
data cursor_list_models_flag: String = "--list-models"
data cursor_invalid_api_key_marker: NonEmptyStr = "The provided API key is invalid" as NonEmptyStr
data cursor_list_models_header: NonEmptyStr = "Available models" as NonEmptyStr

fn cursor_key_probe_command(program: ProgramIdentity) -> ArgvCommand {
  argv_command(program: program, arguments: [cursor_disable_auto_update_flag, cursor_list_models_flag])
}

fn cursor_sandbox_preflight_command(program: ProgramIdentity) -> ArgvCommand {
  argv_command(program: program, arguments: [cursor_disable_auto_update_flag, "sandbox", "run", "printenv", cursor_sandbox_env_var as String])
}

// MODEL IS A PER-INVOCATION ARGUMENT AND THE ROSTER IS AN ACCOUNT FACT. --model takes an identifier
// and --list-models prints what THIS ACCOUNT may use — the subcommand is literally documented as
// 'List available models for this account' — so the set is entitlement-scoped and cannot be modeled
// as a fixed closed enum without going wrong the moment a plan changes.\n\n193 identifiers were
// listed on srv2 on 2026-07-30, spanning several vendors' models behind one CLI. Enumerating them
// here would be a snapshot pretending to be a specification, so the type stays an identifier and
// the rows below name only the ones this repository has a reason to select, each recorded as
// OBSERVED AVAILABLE rather than as guaranteed.\n\ncursor_model_grok_high answers a direct operator
// question: Cursor Grok 4.5 is available on this account as cursor-grok-4.5-high, with a -fast
// sibling. The -fast variants throughout the roster are a latency tier on the same model, which is
// a selection axis rather than a different capability.

type CursorModelId = NonEmptyStr where brand("CursorModelId")

data cursor_model_auto: CursorModelId = "auto" as CursorModelId
data cursor_model_grok_high: CursorModelId = "cursor-grok-4.5-high" as CursorModelId
data cursor_model_grok_high_fast: CursorModelId = "cursor-grok-4.5-high-fast" as CursorModelId
data cursor_observed_model_roster_size: Int = 193

// DELIBERATELY NOT USED: -w/--worktree starts the agent in an isolated git worktree the CLI creates under ~/.cursor/worktrees/<repo>/<name>. This repository's belt already creates and owns attempt worktrees under the dashboard instance's own attempts root, with the branch name and attempt identity bound to the roadmap node.\n\nAccepting the CLI's worktree would hand that ownership to the provider: the location would move outside the instance root the model treats as owned, the naming would stop deriving from the attempt, and teardown would belong to a directory nobody declared. The flag is recorded so the choice is visible rather than looking like an oversight — a later reader finding two worktree mechanisms should know only one is ours.
// THE RUN AND THE CREDENTIAL ARE TWO FACTS. Everything but the key decides the argv; the key decides
// only where the credential travels. Keeping them apart lets a caller whose key is not in hand (a
// spawn that exports it from a custody file in the child, gunbc.roadmap.roadmap_dispatch_actuator) build the
// same argv this module builds, rather than spelling cursor-agent's flags a second time.
type CursorRunShape {
  model: CursorModelId
  mode: CursorExecutionMode
  sandbox: CursorSandboxChoice
  output_format: CursorOutputFormat
  force: Bool
  trust_workspace: Bool
  workspace: FilePath
  prompt: NonEmptyStr
}

type CursorInvocation {
  auth: CursorAuth
  run: CursorRunShape
}

// THE AUTH FIELD WAS NEVER READ, SO BOTH ARMS PRODUCED THE SAME PROCESS. shape_cursor_agent_argv
// built an argv and returned a List of String, and its accompanying note explained at length why
// the key is deliberately absent from that argv — which was true, and which meant a
// CursorApiKeyArgument invocation and a CursorApiKeyEnvironment invocation were byte-identical and
// equally credential-free. The safe arm was justified in prose and never realized: nothing set
// CURSOR_API_KEY, so an invocation shaped from either arm authenticates as nobody.\n\nThe missing
// piece is that an argv is not a process. A process is a program, an argv AND an environment, and a
// credential passed by environment is invisible in a model whose output stops at the argv — there
// was no field for it to appear in, so 'the key is not in the argv' was simultaneously the security
// property and the reason the mechanism did not work.\n\nSo the shape is the whole invocation. The
// argument arm puts the key in argv where the CLI accepts it and where every ps and audit log
// records it; the environment arm puts it in the environment and leaves argv clean. The two are now
// observably different values, which is what makes the safety claim checkable instead of decorative
// — a witness can assert that the environment arm leaks nothing into argv AND that it actually
// binds the variable, and neither assertion was expressible before.

type CursorEnvBinding {
  name: NonEmptyStr
  value: NonEmptyStr
}

type CursorProcessInvocation {
  program: String
  argv: List<String>
  environment: List<CursorEnvBinding>
}

fn cursor_common_argv(program: ProgramIdentity, run: CursorRunShape) -> List<String> {
  concat([program.invocation as String], cursor_common_arguments(run: run))
}

fn cursor_common_arguments(run: CursorRunShape) -> List<String> {
  concat(
    [cursor_disable_auto_update_flag, "--print"],
    concat(
      ["--output-format", cursor_output_format_wire(f: run.output_format)],
      concat(
        ["--model", run.model as String],
        concat(
          cursor_mode_args(mode: run.mode),
          concat(
            cursor_sandbox_args(choice: run.sandbox),
            concat(
              if run.force { ["--force"] } else { [] },
              if run.trust_workspace { ["--trust"] } else { [] },
            ),
          ),
        ),
      ),
    ),
  )
}

fn cursor_tail_argv(run: CursorRunShape) -> List<String> {
  concat(
    ["--workspace", run.workspace as String],
    [run.prompt as String],
  )
}

// The argv of a run whose key reaches the process through CURSOR_API_KEY set by someone else: the
// environment arm's argv, with nothing about the key in it.
fn cursor_environment_authenticated_argv(program: ProgramIdentity, run: CursorRunShape) -> List<String> {
  argv_words(command: cursor_environment_authenticated_command(program: program, run: run))
}

// The same run as a command, so a caller can bind the attempt's runtime environment around it.
fn cursor_environment_authenticated_command(program: ProgramIdentity, run: CursorRunShape) -> ArgvCommand {
  argv_command(program: program, arguments: concat(cursor_common_arguments(run: run), cursor_tail_argv(run: run)))
}

fn shape_cursor_invocation(program: ProgramIdentity, inv: CursorInvocation) -> CursorProcessInvocation {
  match inv.auth {
    CursorApiKeyArgument { key } =>
      CursorProcessInvocation {
        program: program.invocation as String,
        argv: concat(
          cursor_common_argv(program: program, run: inv.run),
          concat(["--api-key", key as String], cursor_tail_argv(run: inv.run)),
        ),
        environment: [],
      }
    CursorApiKeyEnvironment { key } =>
      CursorProcessInvocation {
        program: program.invocation as String,
        argv: cursor_environment_authenticated_argv(program: program, run: inv.run),
        environment: [
          CursorEnvBinding { name: cursor_api_key_env_var, value: key },
        ],
      }
  }
}

// THE AUTOMATION PATH CANNOT MINT THE LEAKY ARM. CursorApiKeyArgument stays in the type because the CLI genuinely offers --api-key and an extdeps model that hid it would be describing a tool that does not exist — representing a surface honestly is not recommending it. But nothing in this repository has a reason to choose it, and leaving both arms equally reachable makes the leak an accident of which constructor someone happened to call.\n\nSo the automation constructor takes the key and sets the environment arm itself. There is no parameter through which a caller can request the argument form, which is the difference between a documented preference and a shape: reaching the leaky arm now requires writing CursorInvocation by hand and naming CursorApiKeyArgument deliberately, which is a visible decision rather than a default.\n\nWHERE THE KEY COMES FROM IS NOT THIS MODULE'S FACT. gunbc.auth.materialized_secret already owns the lifecycle that turns a SecretRef into a scoped credential and its SecretBinding coproduct already has the EnvVar arm this needs — so the intended composition is a materialization bound to EnvVar carrying cursor_api_key_env_var, performed by the caller inside that bracket. Importing it here would invert the layer: which secret store a deployment uses is policy, and an extdeps module that named one would state a requirement the CLI does not have.
fn cursor_automation_invocation(
  key: NonEmptyStr,
  model: CursorModelId,
  mode: CursorExecutionMode,
  sandbox: CursorSandboxChoice,
  output_format: CursorOutputFormat,
  force: Bool,
  trust_workspace: Bool,
  workspace: FilePath,
  prompt: NonEmptyStr,
) -> CursorInvocation {
  CursorInvocation {
    auth: CursorApiKeyEnvironment { key: key },
    run: CursorRunShape {
      model: model,
      mode: mode,
      sandbox: sandbox,
      output_format: output_format,
      force: force,
      trust_workspace: trust_workspace,
      workspace: workspace,
      prompt: prompt,
    },
  }
}

fn cursor_argv_contains(argv: List<String>, token: String) -> Bool {
  any(argv, a => a == token)
}

fn cursor_invocation_binds_env(
  shaped: CursorProcessInvocation,
  name: NonEmptyStr,
) -> Bool {
  any(shaped.environment, b => (b.name as String) == (name as String))
}
